        explanation: "'Into' is the correct preposition in the phrase 'converts X into Y' — meaning to transform or change X so that it becomes Y. 'Convert into' is the standard collocation: 'converts a transformation into a sequence.' 'To' can sometimes replace 'into' in informal usage — 'converts to a sequence' — but 'into' is more precise in formal written and presented communication because it emphasises the transformative process (X becomes something fundamentally different). 'In' would be incorrect — 'converts in a sequence' has no standard grammatical meaning in this context.",
